  planner_response {
    response: "I will create `/lib/context/JournalContext.tsx` as the single client-side engine for our application. It provides:\n1. Google Sign-In and session persistence via Firebase Auth.\n2. Real-time Firestore document sync (entries, memories) isolated per-user under `/users/{userId}/`.\n3. Auto-save integration for journal writing.\n4. Input sanitization (undefined-stripping) to prevent Firestore crashes.\n5. Transaction completeness to persist conversation turns and analysis results safely."
    thinking: "**Confirming Backend Readiness**\n\nI\'ve successfully established the backend API infrastructure. I\'ve built the necessary server-side endpoints following Next.js 15+ App Router principles. I\'ve implemented a robust structure, including a fallback ladder, error recovery, secure parsing, and robust Firebase admin verification. I\'m pleased with the current backend\'s secure and production-ready architecture.\n\n\n**Designing Frontend Context**\n\nNow, I\'m shifting focus to the frontend, aiming for an elegant user experience. I\'m exploring the necessity of custom React hooks and context. A unified context seems like the best approach. I plan to create a `/lib/context/JournalContext.tsx` to handle authentication, entries, memories, insights, and loading indicators, synchronizing with Firestore directly. The `JournalContext` will manage the user, loading state, and the data.\n\n\n"
    modified_response: "I will create `/lib/context/JournalContext.tsx` as the single client-side engine for our application. It provides:\n1. Google Sign-In and session persistence via Firebase Auth.\n2. Real-time Firestore document sync (entries, memories) isolated per-user under `/users/{userId}/`.\n3. Auto-save integration for journal writing.\n4. Input sanitization (undefined-stripping) to prevent Firestore crashes.\n5. Transaction completeness to persist conversation turns and analysis results safely."
