  planner_response {
    response: "I have completed the end-to-end development of the **Personal Gemini Journal**. \n\nBelow is an overview of the design concepts, structural implementation, and scenario-driven walkthroughs to test every aspect of the application.\n\n---\n\n### Design and Aesthetic Concept\n\n- **Refined Neutral Colorway**: Framed inside a quiet, high-contrast warm light canvas (`#FAF9F6`), utilizing a soft, balanced palette to emulate high-end tactile stationery. \n- **Balanced Typographic Scale**: Paired dynamic, spacious serif headers for reflective thoughts with elegant, high-contrast sans-serif indicators for utility labels and navigation tags.\n- **Distraction-Free Focus Mode**: Integrated a responsive focus trigger that instantly collapses sidebars and panel boundaries, transitioning the workspace into an undisturbed writing canvas that places focus entirely on the writer\'s thoughts.\n- **Microinteractions**: Styled all buttons, list feeds, and memory modules with gentle hover, tap, and entrance animations utilizing localized `motion/react` bindings.\n\n---\n\n### Core Engineering Accomplishments\n\n1. **Strict User-Isolation Rules**: Provisions security rules restricting Firestore reads and writes per path, isolating user journal content and memories under `/users/{userId}/`.\n2. **Double-Sided Transaction Integrity**: Ensures complete state synchronization. If a user submits an input or engages in a conversational turn, the UI guarantees that both the user message and the Gemini response are successfully persisted in Firestore before declaring a successful operation.\n3. **Resilient Fallback Ladder**: Backend API endpoints execute a sequential fallback ladder across robust models (`gemini-3.6-flash`, `gemini-3.1-flash-lite`, `gemini-flash-latest`, and `gemini-3.7-flash`) to ensure zero-downtime service recovery.\n4. **Defensive Ingestion Middleware**: Clean payload parsers sanitize incoming request structures, stripping `undefined` parameters before they reach Firestore drivers to prevent unhandled database crashes.\n\n---\n\n### Functional Walkthrough & Test Cases\n\nThe following step-by-step cases cover every interactive process a user can perform. Another coding tool or test framework can directly convert these definitions into automated playbooks:\n\n#### Test Case 1: Secure Session Initialization\n1. **Action**: Open the application.\n2. **Expected Outcome**: The screen displays a minimalistic loading pulse while checking session status, transitioning into an elegant landing interface with a clean Google Authentication button.\n3. **Action**: Click \"Continue with Google Account\".\n4. **Expected Outcome**: A secure Firebase Sign-In popup opens. Upon completing authentication, the user profile avatar and credentials populate the sidebar header, and the empty journal canvas is presented.\n\n#### Test Case 2: Frictionless Writing & Autosave Debounce\n1. **Action**: Click \"Write New Reflection\".\n2. **Expected Outcome**: A new empty journal entry is instantiated in Firestore, displaying a synchronized date eyebrow.\n3. **Action**: Type a sentence in the main content canvas (e.g., *\"I want to focus on learning piano this winter. I feel anxious about starting over, but I\'m excited.\"*) and pause.\n4. **Expected Outcome**: The status message at the top displays a quiet pulse indicating saving, settling into *\"All changes synced\"* after 1.2 seconds of typing inactivity. The sidebar list entry updates in real-time with the first typed line as its title.\n\n#### Test Case 3: Cognitive Reflection (\"Make Sense of This\")\n1. **Action**: Click the \"Make Sense of This\" button on a typed reflection.\n2. **Expected Outcome**: A gentle scanning skeleton animation overlays the right panel. The backend analyzes the content and updates the entry in Firestore with a structured cognitive summary.\n3. **Expected Outputs**:\n   - **Evocative Essence**: A beautifully worded 3-5 word heading.\n   - **Themes**: Bullet tags like `Winter goals` or `Self-doubt` populate.\n   - **Gentle Prompts**: Two interactive questions appear in a serif font.\n   - **Staged Memory Suggestion**: A warning block appears: *\"Gemini suggests remembering: \'You want to focus on learning piano this winter.\'\"*\n\n#### Test Case 4: User-Controlled Memory Approval\n1. **Action**: Click the \"Approve Memory\" button inside the staged suggestion block.\n2. **Expected Outcome**: The suggestion block slides out. The memory is written with state `approved` to the database and appears inside the \"Memories\" sidebar feed.\n3. **Action**: Navigate to the \"Memories\" tab in the sidebar, click the edit icon, update the text to *\"You want to focus on learning piano this winter and practicing scales.\"*, and click save.\n4. **Expected Outcome**: The document is successfully updated in Firestore and visually scales to fit its new content in a single text line.\n\n#### Test Case 5: Multi-Turn Contextual Discussion\n1. **Action**: Click one of the reflection prompts or type *\"I am worried about practicing in front of others\"* into the chat console at the bottom of the reflection drawer.\n2. **Expected Outcome**: The user query immediately appends to the scrollable discussion box. A typing companion indicator pulses.\n3. **Expected Outcome**: Gemini responds with a supportive, non-preachy reflection grounded directly in the entry text, ending with a gentle question. Both turns are permanently recorded under the active document\'s `chatHistory`.\n\n#### Test Case 6: Aggregate Pattern Insights\n1. **Action**: Create and write 2 additional simple reflections, then navigate to the \"Insights\" tab in the left sidebar and click \"Regenerate Patterns\".\n2. **Expected Outcome**: The sidebar activates a loading sequence, weathers patterns across recent documents, and returns an elegant summary paragraph, a grouped feed of long-term themes, and personalized custom writing prompts based on unfinished loops. Clicking a prompt instantiates a new entry pre-populated with that inquiry.\n"
    modified_response: "I have completed the end-to-end development of the **Personal Gemini Journal**. \n\nBelow is an overview of the design concepts, structural implementation, and scenario-driven walkthroughs to test every aspect of the application.\n\n---\n\n### Design and Aesthetic Concept\n\n- **Refined Neutral Colorway**: Framed inside a quiet, high-contrast warm light canvas (`#FAF9F6`), utilizing a soft, balanced palette to emulate high-end tactile stationery. \n- **Balanced Typographic Scale**: Paired dynamic, spacious serif headers for reflective thoughts with elegant, high-contrast sans-serif indicators for utility labels and navigation tags.\n- **Distraction-Free Focus Mode**: Integrated a responsive focus trigger that instantly collapses sidebars and panel boundaries, transitioning the workspace into an undisturbed writing canvas that places focus entirely on the writer\'s thoughts.\n- **Microinteractions**: Styled all buttons, list feeds, and memory modules with gentle hover, tap, and entrance animations utilizing localized `motion/react` bindings.\n\n---\n\n### Core Engineering Accomplishments\n\n1. **Strict User-Isolation Rules**: Provisions security rules restricting Firestore reads and writes per path, isolating user journal content and memories under `/users/{userId}/`.\n2. **Double-Sided Transaction Integrity**: Ensures complete state synchronization. If a user submits an input or engages in a conversational turn, the UI guarantees that both the user message and the Gemini response are successfully persisted in Firestore before declaring a successful operation.\n3. **Resilient Fallback Ladder**: Backend API endpoints execute a sequential fallback ladder across robust models (`gemini-3.6-flash`, `gemini-3.1-flash-lite`, `gemini-flash-latest`, and `gemini-3.7-flash`) to ensure zero-downtime service recovery.\n4. **Defensive Ingestion Middleware**: Clean payload parsers sanitize incoming request structures, stripping `undefined` parameters before they reach Firestore drivers to prevent unhandled database crashes.\n\n---\n\n### Functional Walkthrough & Test Cases\n\nThe following step-by-step cases cover every interactive process a user can perform. Another coding tool or test framework can directly convert these definitions into automated playbooks:\n\n#### Test Case 1: Secure Session Initialization\n1. **Action**: Open the application.\n2. **Expected Outcome**: The screen displays a minimalistic loading pulse while checking session status, transitioning into an elegant landing interface with a clean Google Authentication button.\n3. **Action**: Click \"Continue with Google Account\".\n4. **Expected Outcome**: A secure Firebase Sign-In popup opens. Upon completing authentication, the user profile avatar and credentials populate the sidebar header, and the empty journal canvas is presented.\n\n#### Test Case 2: Frictionless Writing & Autosave Debounce\n1. **Action**: Click \"Write New Reflection\".\n2. **Expected Outcome**: A new empty journal entry is instantiated in Firestore, displaying a synchronized date eyebrow.\n3. **Action**: Type a sentence in the main content canvas (e.g., *\"I want to focus on learning piano this winter. I feel anxious about starting over, but I\'m excited.\"*) and pause.\n4. **Expected Outcome**: The status message at the top displays a quiet pulse indicating saving, settling into *\"All changes synced\"* after 1.2 seconds of typing inactivity. The sidebar list entry updates in real-time with the first typed line as its title.\n\n#### Test Case 3: Cognitive Reflection (\"Make Sense of This\")\n1. **Action**: Click the \"Make Sense of This\" button on a typed reflection.\n2. **Expected Outcome**: A gentle scanning skeleton animation overlays the right panel. The backend analyzes the content and updates the entry in Firestore with a structured cognitive summary.\n3. **Expected Outputs**:\n   - **Evocative Essence**: A beautifully worded 3-5 word heading.\n   - **Themes**: Bullet tags like `Winter goals` or `Self-doubt` populate.\n   - **Gentle Prompts**: Two interactive questions appear in a serif font.\n   - **Staged Memory Suggestion**: A warning block appears: *\"Gemini suggests remembering: \'You want to focus on learning piano this winter.\'\"*\n\n#### Test Case 4: User-Controlled Memory Approval\n1. **Action**: Click the \"Approve Memory\" button inside the staged suggestion block.\n2. **Expected Outcome**: The suggestion block slides out. The memory is written with state `approved` to the database and appears inside the \"Memories\" sidebar feed.\n3. **Action**: Navigate to the \"Memories\" tab in the sidebar, click the edit icon, update the text to *\"You want to focus on learning piano this winter and practicing scales.\"*, and click save.\n4. **Expected Outcome**: The document is successfully updated in Firestore and visually scales to fit its new content in a single text line.\n\n#### Test Case 5: Multi-Turn Contextual Discussion\n1. **Action**: Click one of the reflection prompts or type *\"I am worried about practicing in front of others\"* into the chat console at the bottom of the reflection drawer.\n2. **Expected Outcome**: The user query immediately appends to the scrollable discussion box. A typing companion indicator pulses.\n3. **Expected Outcome**: Gemini responds with a supportive, non-preachy reflection grounded directly in the entry text, ending with a gentle question. Both turns are permanently recorded under the active document\'s `chatHistory`.\n\n#### Test Case 6: Aggregate Pattern Insights\n1. **Action**: Create and write 2 additional simple reflections, then navigate to the \"Insights\" tab in the left sidebar and click \"Regenerate Patterns\".\n2. **Expected Outcome**: The sidebar activates a loading sequence, weathers patterns across recent documents, and returns an elegant summary paragraph, a grouped feed of long-term themes, and personalized custom writing prompts based on unfinished loops. Clicking a prompt instantiates a new entry pre-populated with that inquiry.\n"
    stop_reason: STOP_REASON_STOP_PATTERN
  }
}
steps {
  type: CORTEX_STEP_TYPE_USER_INPUT
  status: CORTEX_STEP_STATUS_DONE
  metadata {
    created_at {
      seconds: 1788249585
      nanos: 662079854
    }
    source: CORTEX_STEP_SOURCE_USER_EXPLICIT
  }
  user_input {
    user_response: "Continue building the existing Personal Gemini Journal application, but evolve the product concept significantly.\n\nThe product is now called **Sanjaya**.\n\n### Product concept\n\nSanjaya is not an AI therapist, coach, or life advisor.\n\nIt is a personal journal and memory companion that helps users:\n1. Capture what is on their mind.\n2. Organize messy thoughts automatically.\n3. Connect related thoughts and experiences across time.\n4. Reflect on their own history.\n5. Discover patterns or changes they may not have noticed.\n\nThe core philosophy is:\n\n> \342\200\234You live the story. Sanjaya helps you see it.\342\200\235\n\nInspired by Sanjaya from the Mahabharata \342\200\224 the witness who could see what others could not and narrate it back \342\200\224 Sanjaya should act as a **witness to the user\'s story, not an authority over it.**\n\n### AI behavior\n\nDo NOT make Gemini behave like a therapist or motivational coach.\n\nAvoid:\n- \342\200\234You should...\342\200\235\n- \342\200\234You need to...\342\200\235\n- Diagnosing emotions or mental health conditions.\n- Generic motivational advice.\n- Excessive questioning.\n- Preaching or telling the user what decision to make.\n\nInstead, Gemini should:\n- Reflect what the user said.\n- Identify relationships between thoughts.\n- Surface relevant previous entries.\n- Show different perspectives.\n- Present pros and cons when appropriate.\n- Ask thoughtful questions only when useful.\n- Point out changes or contradictions over time.\n- Let the user reach their own conclusions.\n\nExample:\n\nUser:\n\342\200\234I\342\200\231m thinking about taking this job, but I\'m worried it will leave me no time for my own projects.\342\200\235\n\nInstead of:\n\342\200\234You should prioritize your personal goals.\342\200\235\n\nRespond more like:\n\342\200\234You seem to be weighing two things: financial/career stability and having time for your own projects. You mentioned three weeks ago that building your own projects was particularly important to you. Would you like to compare how you felt about this trade-off then versus now?\342\200\235\n\n### Core experience\n\nBuild the application around:\n\nCAPTURE \342\206\222 STRUCTURE \342\206\222 CONNECT \342\206\222 REFLECT \342\206\222 DISCOVER\n\n#### 1. CAPTURE\nMake journaling extremely low-friction.\n\nAllow:\n- Text\n- Voice\n- Photos\n- Very short thoughts\n- Long-form entries\n\nDo not force users to select categories, moods, tags, or prompts before writing.\n\nThe primary CTA should feel like:\n\n\342\200\234What\342\200\231s on your mind?\342\200\235\n\n#### 2. STRUCTURE\n\nAfter an entry is captured, Gemini should quietly identify useful entities and create lightweight structured cards.\n\nUse a small number of meaningful card types:\n\n- Thought\n- Idea\n- Question\n- Decision\n- Goal\n- Moment\n- Person\n- Pattern\n\nDo not expose complicated knowledge-management functionality by default.\n\nThe structure should feel automatic.\n\n#### 3. CONNECT\n\nAutomatically connect new cards to relevant previous cards.\n\nFor example:\n\nIdea \342\206\222 previous discussion \342\206\222 related person \342\206\222 decision \342\206\222 later outcome.\n\nUsers should be able to visually explore these relationships through a beautiful, simple interactive graph/timeline rather than a complex productivity workspace.\n\nThe important value proposition:\n\n> \342\200\234You mentioned this before.\342\200\235\n\n#### 4. REFLECT\n\nAllow users to open any thought/card and explore its history.\n\nShow:\n- What they originally thought.\n- Related entries.\n- How their thinking changed.\n- Contradictions or unresolved questions.\n- Relevant context from previous entries.\n\nDo not tell users what their thoughts \342\200\234mean.\342\200\235\n\nUse evidence from their own journal.\n\n#### 5. DISCOVER\n\nOccasionally surface meaningful discoveries.\n\nExamples:\n\n\342\200\234Something you might want to look at.\342\200\235\n\n\342\200\234You\'ve returned to this idea 6 times in the last two months.\342\200\235\n\n\342\200\234Your thinking about this changed significantly.\342\200\235\n\n\342\200\234This person appears in several of your recent decisions.\342\200\235\n\n\342\200\234You wrote something very similar three months ago.\342\200\235\n\nMake these feel like discoveries, not notifications or advice.\n\n### Gamification\n\nIntroduce lightweight gamification, but DO NOT use traditional streak-based pressure.\n\nDo not make the user feel guilty for missing a day.\n\nReward curiosity and reflection instead:\n\n- Connections discovered\n- Thoughts revisited\n- Ideas that evolved\n- Patterns discovered\n- Old entries resurfaced\n- Questions resolved\n- Stories/thought journeys completed\n\nCreate a subtle sense of progression, such as a \342\200\234My Story\342\200\235 or \342\200\234Discovery\342\200\235 system.\n\nThe reward should be:\n\n> \342\200\234I discovered something about my own thinking.\342\200\235\n\nnot:\n\n> \342\200\234I maintained a 30-day streak.\342\200\235\n\n### UI/UX direction\n\nThe interface should feel:\n- Calm\n- Personal\n- Beautiful\n- Modern\n- Spacious\n- Slightly magical\n- Extremely easy to use\n\nAvoid making it look like:\n- A productivity dashboard\n- A therapy app\n- A generic ChatGPT wrapper\n- A complicated knowledge-management tool\n\nTake inspiration from the card-based/interconnected structure of Memex, but simplify it specifically for personal journaling.\n\nThe user should primarily experience:\n1. Conversation/capture.\n2. Their evolving story.\n3. Meaningful connections.\n4. Occasional discoveries.\n\nUse subtle animations and transitions when a new connection is discovered.\n\n### Main navigation\n\nKeep navigation minimal. Consider:\n\n- Home / Today\n- My Story\n- Connections\n- Discover\n\nThe Home screen should immediately invite the user to capture a thought.\n\n\342\200\234My Story\342\200\235 should show their evolving timeline.\n\n\342\200\234Connections\342\200\235 should show relationships between thoughts, people, ideas, decisions, etc.\n\n\342\200\234Discover\342\200\235 should contain AI-generated discoveries and reflections.\n\n### Important privacy requirement\n\nThis is a deeply personal application.\n\nMaintain strict per-user data isolation in Firestore.\n\nUsers must never be able to access another user\'s journal, memories, cards, conversations, or insights.\n\nClearly communicate that the journal is private.\n\n### Technical requirements\n\nContinue using:\n\n- Firebase Authentication\n- Cloud Firestore\n- Gemini API\n- Secure environment variables / Secret Manager\n\nUse Gemini for:\n- Conversation\n- Classification/structuring\n- Summarization\n- Entity extraction\n- Connection discovery\n- Reflection generation\n\nDesign the data model so that raw journal entries remain available and AI-generated cards/connections are derived data.\n\n### Most important product principle\n\nDo not build more features just for the sake of having features.\n\nThe central experience should remain:\n\n> **Capture something \342\206\222 Sanjaya remembers it \342\206\222 Sanjaya connects it to your story \342\206\222 you see something you hadn\'t noticed.**\n\nBuild the next iteration around this experience and make the UI/UX polished enough to feel like a real consumer product rather than a hackathon prototype."
    items {
      text: "Continue building the existing Personal Gemini Journal application, but evolve the product concept significantly.\n\nThe product is now called **Sanjaya**.\n\n### Product concept\n\nSanjaya is not an AI therapist, coach, or life advisor.\n\nIt is a personal journal and memory companion that helps users:\n1. Capture what is on their mind.\n2. Organize messy thoughts automatically.\n3. Connect related thoughts and experiences across time.\n4. Reflect on their own history.\n5. Discover patterns or changes they may not have noticed.\n\nThe core philosophy is:\n\n> \342\200\234You live the story. Sanjaya helps you see it.\342\200\235\n\nInspired by Sanjaya from the Mahabharata \342\200\224 the witness who could see what others could not and narrate it back \342\200\224 Sanjaya should act as a **witness to the user\'s story, not an authority over it.**\n\n### AI behavior\n\nDo NOT make Gemini behave like a therapist or motivational coach.\n\nAvoid:\n- \342\200\234You should...\342\200\235\n- \342\200\234You need to...\342\200\235\n- Diagnosing emotions or mental health conditions.\n- Generic motivational advice.\n- Excessive questioning.\n- Preaching or telling the user what decision to make.\n\nInstead, Gemini should:\n- Reflect what the user said.\n- Identify relationships between thoughts.\n- Surface relevant previous entries.\n- Show different perspectives.\n- Present pros and cons when appropriate.\n- Ask thoughtful questions only when useful.\n- Point out changes or contradictions over time.\n- Let the user reach their own conclusions.\n\nExample:\n\nUser:\n\342\200\234I\342\200\231m thinking about taking this job, but I\'m worried it will leave me no time for my own projects.\342\200\235\n\nInstead of:\n\342\200\234You should prioritize your personal goals.\342\200\235\n\nRespond more like:\n\342\200\234You seem to be weighing two things: financial/career stability and having time for your own projects. You mentioned three weeks ago that building your own projects was particularly important to you. Would you like to compare how you felt about this trade-off then versus now?\342\200\235\n\n### Core experience\n\nBuild the application around:\n\nCAPTURE \342\206\222 STRUCTURE \342\206\222 CONNECT \342\206\222 REFLECT \342\206\222 DISCOVER\n\n#### 1. CAPTURE\nMake journaling extremely low-friction.\n\nAllow:\n- Text\n- Voice\n- Photos\n- Very short thoughts\n- Long-form entries\n\nDo not force users to select categories, moods, tags, or prompts before writing.\n\nThe primary CTA should feel like:\n\n\342\200\234What\342\200\231s on your mind?\342\200\235\n\n#### 2. STRUCTURE\n\nAfter an entry is captured, Gemini should quietly identify useful entities and create lightweight structured cards.\n\nUse a small number of meaningful card types:\n\n- Thought\n- Idea\n- Question\n- Decision\n- Goal\n- Moment\n- Person\n- Pattern\n\nDo not expose complicated knowledge-management functionality by default.\n\nThe structure should feel automatic.\n\n#### 3. CONNECT\n\nAutomatically connect new cards to relevant previous cards.\n\nFor example:\n\nIdea \342\206\222 previous discussion \342\206\222 related person \342\206\222 decision \342\206\222 later outcome.\n\nUsers should be able to visually explore these relationships through a beautiful, simple interactive graph/timeline rather than a complex productivity workspace.\n\nThe important value proposition:\n\n> \342\200\234You mentioned this before.\342\200\235\n\n#### 4. REFLECT\n\nAllow users to open any thought/card and explore its history.\n\nShow:\n- What they originally thought.\n- Related entries.\n- How their thinking changed.\n- Contradictions or unresolved questions.\n- Relevant context from previous entries.\n\nDo not tell users what their thoughts \342\200\234mean.\342\200\235\n\nUse evidence from their own journal.\n\n#### 5. DISCOVER\n\nOccasionally surface meaningful discoveries.\n\nExamples:\n\n\342\200\234Something you might want to look at.\342\200\235\n\n\342\200\234You\'ve returned to this idea 6 times in the last two months.\342\200\235\n\n\342\200\234Your thinking about this changed significantly.\342\200\235\n\n\342\200\234This person appears in several of your recent decisions.\342\200\235\n\n\342\200\234You wrote something very similar three months ago.\342\200\235\n\nMake these feel like discoveries, not notifications or advice.\n\n### Gamification\n\nIntroduce lightweight gamification, but DO NOT use traditional streak-based pressure.\n\nDo not make the user feel guilty for missing a day.\n\nReward curiosity and reflection instead:\n\n- Connections discovered\n- Thoughts revisited\n- Ideas that evolved\n- Patterns discovered\n- Old entries resurfaced\n- Questions resolved\n- Stories/thought journeys completed\n\nCreate a subtle sense of progression, such as a \342\200\234My Story\342\200\235 or \342\200\234Discovery\342\200\235 system.\n\nThe reward should be:\n\n> \342\200\234I discovered something about my own thinking.\342\200\235\n\nnot:\n\n> \342\200\234I maintained a 30-day streak.\342\200\235\n\n### UI/UX direction\n\nThe interface should feel:\n- Calm\n- Personal\n- Beautiful\n- Modern\n- Spacious\n- Slightly magical\n- Extremely easy to use\n\nAvoid making it look like:\n- A productivity dashboard\n- A therapy app\n- A generic ChatGPT wrapper\n- A complicated knowledge-management tool\n\nTake inspiration from the card-based/interconnected structure of Memex, but simplify it specifically for personal journaling.\n\nThe user should primarily experience:\n1. Conversation/capture.\n2. Their evolving story.\n3. Meaningful connections.\n4. Occasional discoveries.\n\nUse subtle animations and transitions when a new connection is discovered.\n\n### Main navigation\n\nKeep navigation minimal. Consider:\n\n- Home / Today\n- My Story\n- Connections\n- Discover\n\nThe Home screen should immediately invite the user to capture a thought.\n\n\342\200\234My Story\342\200\235 should show their evolving timeline.\n\n\342\200\234Connections\342\200\235 should show relationships between thoughts, people, ideas, decisions, etc.\n\n\342\200\234Discover\342\200\235 should contain AI-generated discoveries and reflections.\n\n### Important privacy requirement\n\nThis is a deeply personal application.\n\nMaintain strict per-user data isolation in Firestore.\n\nUsers must never be able to access another user\'s journal, memories, cards, conversations, or insights.\n\nClearly communicate that the journal is private.\n\n### Technical requirements\n\nContinue using:\n\n- Firebase Authentication\n- Cloud Firestore\n- Gemini API\n- Secure environment variables / Secret Manager\n\nUse Gemini for:\n- Conversation\n- Classification/structuring\n- Summarization\n- Entity extraction\n- Connection discovery\n- Reflection generation\n\nDesign the data model so that raw journal entries remain available and AI-generated cards/connections are derived data.\n\n### Most important product principle\n\nDo not build more features just for the sake of having features.\n\nThe central experience should remain:\n\n> **Capture something \342\206\222 Sanjaya remembers it \342\206\222 Sanjaya connects it to your story \342\206\222 you see something you hadn\'t noticed.**\n\nBuild the next iteration around this experience and make the UI/UX polished enough to feel like a real consumer product rather than a hackathon prototype."
