  planner_response {
    response: "I will create `firebase-blueprint.json` to define the intermediate data schema blueprint for the personal memory engine."
    thinking: "**Analyzing the Personal Memory Engine**\n\nI\'m deep diving into the user\'s detailed specification for the Personal Memory Engine, specifically the backend architecture. I\'ve noted the core philosophy: the system remembers more than it verbalizes and the immutability of raw user input, with derived information added. This foundational understanding is crucial for the subsequent steps.\n\n\n**Defining Memory Components**\n\nI\'ve been mapping out the key components within the memory model. I am now focused on classifying memory types. I am breaking memories into Experiences/Episodes with attached Media. Temporal aspects are critical, with precise time resolution. I\'m exploring provenance for each memory component. Relationships, entities, emotions, internal states, and learnings are all integrated. The reconciliation mechanisms like CREATE, UPDATE, and LINK are also being evaluated.\n\n\n**Mapping API Endpoints**\n\nNow, I\'m analyzing the provided API endpoints. It looks like `POST /api/v1/capture/media` handles media uploads, while `GET /api/v1/captures/{id}` provides capture status.  I see endpoints for entities, relationships, timeline retrieval, and hybrid memory access.  I\'m also looking at reflective conversational capabilities via `/api/v1/reflect` and user correction features.\n\n\n**Evaluating Security and Robustness**\n\nI\'m now deep into the security aspects. Authorization is consistently applied across all API boundaries. Output encoding is handled meticulously. I\'ve noted the secure Firestore implementation, with user data isolation, and strict undefined-stripping. I\'ve focused on zero secret hardcoding. The Gemini model\'s resilience, with its fallback protocol, is also being mapped out. I\'m scrutinizing the server-side robustness and payload ingestion standards. I will soon be developing comprehensive user walkthroughs and test cases. I\'m checking the database and server architecture in this Next.js project. I believe we can implement the full suite of routes in Next.js.\n\n\n**Enumerating API Routes**\n\nI\'ve just finished reviewing the detailed breakdown of all API routes, including those for capture, entities, relationships, retrieval, reflection, memories, and maintenance. The Next.js file structure clearly delineates the handling of GET, POST, PATCH, and DELETE requests for each resource.\n\nI\'m now diving into the core engine services. I\'m focusing on the type definitions and data extraction services within the `/lib/memory-engine/` directory. I\'m focusing on key data structures, such as CaptureSession, MediaAsset, Episode, Entity, Person, Relationship, Emotion, MemoryState, Learning, Pattern, Knowledge, Fact, Decision, Question, Expectation, Outcome, Significance, MemoryProvenance, and RetrievalContext. I\'m particularly interested in the multimodal LLM extraction capabilities.\n\n\n**Examining Core Engine Services**\n\nI\'m now zeroing in on the architecture of core engine services, specifically how Gemini is used with schema enforcement. I see entity resolution, temporal parsing, and emotional attribution are key aspects. I\'ve begun reviewing the `/lib/memory-engine/reconciliation.ts` file, and am focusing on the state reconciler that implements the rules for `CREATE`, `UPDATE`, `LINK`, `CORRECT`, `SUPERSEDE`, and `IGNORE`. I\'m also going through the `retriever.ts` for the retrieval engine, and checking `reflection.ts` for the conversational reflection engine. Finally, I\'m noting the maintenance module for pattern detection.\n\n\n"
    modified_response: "I will create `firebase-blueprint.json` to define the intermediate data schema blueprint for the personal memory engine."
