    source: CORTEX_STEP_SOURCE_USER_EXPLICIT
  }
  user_input {
    user_response: "# Backend Update: Memory Contradiction & Clarification Layer\n\nThis is an **incremental update** to the existing backend architecture. Do not rewrite or replace the existing architecture. Inspect the current implementation and integrate this capability into the existing memory pipeline.\n\n## Goal\n\nThe journal should not blindly treat every statement from the user as fact.\n\nIt should be able to recognize when something the user says is:\n\n* ambiguous\n* potentially incorrect\n* inconsistent with known world knowledge\n* inconsistent with existing personal memory\n* referring to the wrong entity\n* temporally inconsistent\n\nWhen the system has strong evidence that something may be wrong, it should **gently ask for clarification** rather than silently correcting the user.\n\nThe interaction should feel like a thoughtful friend, not a fact-checker.\n\n---\n\n## Example\n\nUser:\n\n> \"I watched Friends today and loved the Halloween episode where Sheldon, Leonard, Raj and Howard try to scare Sheldon.\"\n\nThe system should recognize that:\n\n* Sheldon\n* Leonard\n* Raj\n* Howard\n\nare associated with **The Big Bang Theory**, creating a likely mismatch with \"Friends\".\n\nInstead of storing \"Friends\" as unquestioned truth, generate a clarification:\n\n> \"Do you mean The Big Bang Theory? Sheldon, Leonard, Raj and Howard are from that show.\"\n\nThe user can then respond:\n\n> \"Yes.\"\n\nThe system should store the corrected interpretation as the active memory:\n\n```text\nMedia:\nThe Big Bang Theory\n\nExperience:\nUser watched the Halloween episode\n\nReaction:\nUser loved it / found it hilarious\n\nCharacters:\nSheldon\nLeonard\nRaj\nHoward\n\nDate:\nToday\n```\n\nBut the original input must remain preserved as provenance.\n\n---\n\n## Add a Clarification Layer\n\nExtend the existing memory pipeline:\n\n```text\nUser Input\n   \342\206\223\nExisting Multimodal Processing\n   \342\206\223\nExisting Memory Extraction\n   \342\206\223\nExisting Entity / Temporal Resolution\n   \342\206\223\nNEW: Consistency Check\n   \342\206\223\n \342\224\214\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\220\n \342\224\202                       \342\224\202\nNo meaningful issue    Possible conflict\n \342\224\202                       \342\224\202\n \342\206\223                       \342\206\223\nPersist              Clarification\n                         \342\206\223\n                    User response\n                         \342\206\223\n                 Memory reconciliation\n                         \342\206\223\n                      Persist\n```\n\nDo not create an unnecessary autonomous agent for this.\n\nImplement it as part of the existing memory orchestration/reconciliation layer.\n\n---\n\n## Important: Distinguish These Cases\n\n### 1. Ambiguity\n\nIf the user says:\n\n> \"I met Sam.\"\n\nand multiple people called Sam exist, the system should not arbitrarily choose one.\n\nAsk:\n\n> \"Which Sam do you mean?\"\n\nOnly ask when the ambiguity materially affects the memory.\n\n---\n\n### 2. Entity mismatch\n\nExample:\n\n> \"I watched Friends with Sheldon.\"\n\nUse world knowledge/context to detect that this may refer to The Big Bang Theory.\n\nAsk for confirmation.\n\n---\n\n### 3. Temporal inconsistency\n\nDo not flag every date difference.\n\nExample:\n\n> \"I met CU at Google yesterday.\"\n\nEven if the person\'s current employer is Microsoft, this may still be perfectly valid if they previously worked at Google or the meeting happened in a Google context.\n\nTemporal reasoning must consider historical validity.\n\n---\n\n### 4. Multiple valid relationships\n\nIf the memory says:\n\n```text\nRahul \342\206\222 brother\n```\n\nand the user says:\n\n> \"Rahul is also my colleague.\"\n\nThis is NOT a contradiction.\n\nAdd:\n\n```text\nRahul \342\206\222 brother\nRahul \342\206\222 colleague\n```\n\nDo not replace the first relationship.\n\n---\n\n### 5. User correction\n\nIf the user says:\n\n> \"Actually, I met Rahul Thursday, not Wednesday.\"\n\nUpdate the active memory to Thursday while preserving:\n\n```text\nOriginal:\nWednesday\n\nCorrection:\nThursday\n```\n\nThe original capture must remain immutable.\n\n---\n\n## Confidence and Interruption\n\nDo not ask the user about every possible inconsistency.\n\nUse confidence and significance.\n\nHigh-confidence + meaningful contradiction:\n\n```text\n\342\206\222 ask\n```\n\nLow-confidence or insignificant difference:\n\n```text\n\342\206\222 don\'t interrupt\n```\n\nFor example:\n\n> \"I arrived around 7.\"\n\nwhile metadata says 7:07.\n\nDo NOT ask about this.\n\nThe system should optimize for **useful clarification, not maximum fact checking**.\n\n---\n\n## Epistemic Separation\n\nThe memory system must distinguish:\n\n```text\nUSER SAID\nSYSTEM KNOWS\nSYSTEM INFERRED\nSYSTEM SUSPECTS\nUSER CONFIRMED\nUSER CORRECTED\n```\n\nDo not turn an inference into a fact.\n\nDo not let world knowledge silently overwrite a user\'s memory.\n\nThe user always gets the final say.\n\n---\n\n## Provenance\n\nEvery correction must remain traceable.\n\nExample:\n\n```text\nOriginal capture\n\"I watched Friends...\"\n\n        \342\206\223\n\nSystem clarification\n\"Do you mean The Big Bang Theory?\"\n\n        \342\206\223\n\nUser\n\"Yes.\"\n\n        \342\206\223\n\nActive memory\n\"The Big Bang Theory\"\n```\n\nThe system should be able to trace the active memory back to the original capture and clarification.\n\n---\n\n## Voice Support\n\nThis must work regardless of input type.\n\nThe original input can be:\n\n* voice\n* text\n* photo\n* video\n\nThe clarification can also be conversational voice.\n\nExample:\n\nUser:\n\n> \"I watched Friends...\"\n\nAssistant:\n\n> \"Do you mean The Big Bang Theory? Sheldon and Leonard are from that show.\"\n\nUser:\n\n> \"Yeah.\"\n\nThen reconcile and store the corrected memory.\n\n---\n\n## UI Contract\n\nExpose clarification candidates to the frontend through the existing API/state architecture.\n\nThe frontend should be able to render:\n\n```text\nclarification\nmessage\ncandidate options\nstatus\nsource capture\n```\n\nThe user should be able to:\n\n* confirm\n* reject\n* provide a different correction\n* dismiss\n\nDo not force the user to select from predefined options; natural-language responses must also work.\n\n---\n\n## Required Tests\n\nAdd tests for:\n\n1. \"Friends + Sheldon\" \342\206\222 clarification\n2. User confirms \342\206\222 corrected active memory\n3. User rejects \342\206\222 preserve original interpretation\n4. Multiple Sams \342\206\222 clarification\n5. Rahul = brother + colleague \342\206\222 no contradiction\n6. Wednesday \342\206\222 Thursday correction\n7. Small time discrepancy \342\206\222 no clarification\n8. Historical employer change \342\206\222 no false contradiction\n9. Original input remains preserved\n10. Voice and text use the same clarification mechanism\n\n---\n\n## Guiding Principle\n\nThe system should behave like:\n\n> **A careful friend who remembers.**\n\nNot:\n\n> **A fact checker who corrects.**\n\nThe desired behavior is:\n\n**Notice \342\206\222 Question \342\206\222 Confirm \342\206\222 Reconcile \342\206\222 Remember.**\n\nNever:\n\n**Notice \342\206\222 Assume \342\206\222 Overwrite.**"
    items {
      text: "# Backend Update: Memory Contradiction & Clarification Layer\n\nThis is an **incremental update** to the existing backend architecture. Do not rewrite or replace the existing architecture. Inspect the current implementation and integrate this capability into the existing memory pipeline.\n\n## Goal\n\nThe journal should not blindly treat every statement from the user as fact.\n\nIt should be able to recognize when something the user says is:\n\n* ambiguous\n* potentially incorrect\n* inconsistent with known world knowledge\n* inconsistent with existing personal memory\n* referring to the wrong entity\n* temporally inconsistent\n\nWhen the system has strong evidence that something may be wrong, it should **gently ask for clarification** rather than silently correcting the user.\n\nThe interaction should feel like a thoughtful friend, not a fact-checker.\n\n---\n\n## Example\n\nUser:\n\n> \"I watched Friends today and loved the Halloween episode where Sheldon, Leonard, Raj and Howard try to scare Sheldon.\"\n\nThe system should recognize that:\n\n* Sheldon\n* Leonard\n* Raj\n* Howard\n\nare associated with **The Big Bang Theory**, creating a likely mismatch with \"Friends\".\n\nInstead of storing \"Friends\" as unquestioned truth, generate a clarification:\n\n> \"Do you mean The Big Bang Theory? Sheldon, Leonard, Raj and Howard are from that show.\"\n\nThe user can then respond:\n\n> \"Yes.\"\n\nThe system should store the corrected interpretation as the active memory:\n\n```text\nMedia:\nThe Big Bang Theory\n\nExperience:\nUser watched the Halloween episode\n\nReaction:\nUser loved it / found it hilarious\n\nCharacters:\nSheldon\nLeonard\nRaj\nHoward\n\nDate:\nToday\n```\n\nBut the original input must remain preserved as provenance.\n\n---\n\n## Add a Clarification Layer\n\nExtend the existing memory pipeline:\n\n```text\nUser Input\n   \342\206\223\nExisting Multimodal Processing\n   \342\206\223\nExisting Memory Extraction\n   \342\206\223\nExisting Entity / Temporal Resolution\n   \342\206\223\nNEW: Consistency Check\n   \342\206\223\n \342\224\214\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\220\n \342\224\202                       \342\224\202\nNo meaningful issue    Possible conflict\n \342\224\202                       \342\224\202\n \342\206\223                       \342\206\223\nPersist              Clarification\n                         \342\206\223\n                    User response\n                         \342\206\223\n                 Memory reconciliation\n                         \342\206\223\n                      Persist\n```\n\nDo not create an unnecessary autonomous agent for this.\n\nImplement it as part of the existing memory orchestration/reconciliation layer.\n\n---\n\n## Important: Distinguish These Cases\n\n### 1. Ambiguity\n\nIf the user says:\n\n> \"I met Sam.\"\n\nand multiple people called Sam exist, the system should not arbitrarily choose one.\n\nAsk:\n\n> \"Which Sam do you mean?\"\n\nOnly ask when the ambiguity materially affects the memory.\n\n---\n\n### 2. Entity mismatch\n\nExample:\n\n> \"I watched Friends with Sheldon.\"\n\nUse world knowledge/context to detect that this may refer to The Big Bang Theory.\n\nAsk for confirmation.\n\n---\n\n### 3. Temporal inconsistency\n\nDo not flag every date difference.\n\nExample:\n\n> \"I met CU at Google yesterday.\"\n\nEven if the person\'s current employer is Microsoft, this may still be perfectly valid if they previously worked at Google or the meeting happened in a Google context.\n\nTemporal reasoning must consider historical validity.\n\n---\n\n### 4. Multiple valid relationships\n\nIf the memory says:\n\n```text\nRahul \342\206\222 brother\n```\n\nand the user says:\n\n> \"Rahul is also my colleague.\"\n\nThis is NOT a contradiction.\n\nAdd:\n\n```text\nRahul \342\206\222 brother\nRahul \342\206\222 colleague\n```\n\nDo not replace the first relationship.\n\n---\n\n### 5. User correction\n\nIf the user says:\n\n> \"Actually, I met Rahul Thursday, not Wednesday.\"\n\nUpdate the active memory to Thursday while preserving:\n\n```text\nOriginal:\nWednesday\n\nCorrection:\nThursday\n```\n\nThe original capture must remain immutable.\n\n---\n\n## Confidence and Interruption\n\nDo not ask the user about every possible inconsistency.\n\nUse confidence and significance.\n\nHigh-confidence + meaningful contradiction:\n\n```text\n\342\206\222 ask\n```\n\nLow-confidence or insignificant difference:\n\n```text\n\342\206\222 don\'t interrupt\n```\n\nFor example:\n\n> \"I arrived around 7.\"\n\nwhile metadata says 7:07.\n\nDo NOT ask about this.\n\nThe system should optimize for **useful clarification, not maximum fact checking**.\n\n---\n\n## Epistemic Separation\n\nThe memory system must distinguish:\n\n```text\nUSER SAID\nSYSTEM KNOWS\nSYSTEM INFERRED\nSYSTEM SUSPECTS\nUSER CONFIRMED\nUSER CORRECTED\n```\n\nDo not turn an inference into a fact.\n\nDo not let world knowledge silently overwrite a user\'s memory.\n\nThe user always gets the final say.\n\n---\n\n## Provenance\n\nEvery correction must remain traceable.\n\nExample:\n\n```text\nOriginal capture\n\"I watched Friends...\"\n\n        \342\206\223\n\nSystem clarification\n\"Do you mean The Big Bang Theory?\"\n\n        \342\206\223\n\nUser\n\"Yes.\"\n\n        \342\206\223\n\nActive memory\n\"The Big Bang Theory\"\n```\n\nThe system should be able to trace the active memory back to the original capture and clarification.\n\n---\n\n## Voice Support\n\nThis must work regardless of input type.\n\nThe original input can be:\n\n* voice\n* text\n* photo\n* video\n\nThe clarification can also be conversational voice.\n\nExample:\n\nUser:\n\n> \"I watched Friends...\"\n\nAssistant:\n\n> \"Do you mean The Big Bang Theory? Sheldon and Leonard are from that show.\"\n\nUser:\n\n> \"Yeah.\"\n\nThen reconcile and store the corrected memory.\n\n---\n\n## UI Contract\n\nExpose clarification candidates to the frontend through the existing API/state architecture.\n\nThe frontend should be able to render:\n\n```text\nclarification\nmessage\ncandidate options\nstatus\nsource capture\n```\n\nThe user should be able to:\n\n* confirm\n* reject\n* provide a different correction\n* dismiss\n\nDo not force the user to select from predefined options; natural-language responses must also work.\n\n---\n\n## Required Tests\n\nAdd tests for:\n\n1. \"Friends + Sheldon\" \342\206\222 clarification\n2. User confirms \342\206\222 corrected active memory\n3. User rejects \342\206\222 preserve original interpretation\n4. Multiple Sams \342\206\222 clarification\n5. Rahul = brother + colleague \342\206\222 no contradiction\n6. Wednesday \342\206\222 Thursday correction\n7. Small time discrepancy \342\206\222 no clarification\n8. Historical employer change \342\206\222 no false contradiction\n9. Original input remains preserved\n10. Voice and text use the same clarification mechanism\n\n---\n\n## Guiding Principle\n\nThe system should behave like:\n\n> **A careful friend who remembers.**\n\nNot:\n\n> **A fact checker who corrects.**\n\nThe desired behavior is:\n\n**Notice \342\206\222 Question \342\206\222 Confirm \342\206\222 Reconcile \342\206\222 Remember.**\n\nNever:\n\n**Notice \342\206\222 Assume \342\206\222 Overwrite.**"
